STIMULI = [
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P1", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "For much of the twentieth century, ecologists treated the forest floor as a passive recipient of whatever fell from the canopy: "
        "leaves, branches and the occasional carcass, all broken down at a pace set by temperature and moisture. Studies of decomposition "
        "accordingly focused on climate, and models predicted that leaf litter would decay at similar rates in any two forests with similar weather.\n\n"
        "Field results repeatedly defied those predictions. Litter from the same tree species, placed in different forests with nearly identical "
        "climates, decayed at markedly different rates. One explanation, now called the home-field advantage hypothesis, holds that the fungi and "
        "bacteria beneath a given tree species become specialized in breaking down that species' litter. Transplanted litter, on this view, decays "
        "slowly not because the new site is colder or drier but because the local decomposers are unaccustomed to it. The hypothesis does not "
        "dismiss climate; rather, it suggests that climate sets the outer limits of decomposition while the history of a site determines where "
        "within those limits the actual rate falls.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P2", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Historians of the book have long assumed that the shift from manuscript to print in fifteenth-century Europe was abrupt: once presses "
        "spread, scribes lost their trade within a generation. Surviving account books from several monasteries tell a more gradual story. "
        "Scribes continued to be paid for copying well into the sixteenth century, and some printed books were deliberately finished by hand, "
        "with painted initials and margins designed to resemble those of manuscripts. Buyers, it seems, did not regard the two technologies as "
        "rivals. A wealthy patron might even commission a handwritten copy of a text precisely because printed versions had made that text "
        "widely available, and therefore unremarkable.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P3", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Economists have long noted that people often value an object more once they own it, a pattern called the endowment effect. In a "
        "typical experiment, half the participants receive a mug and are asked the lowest price at which they would sell it; the other half "
        "are asked the highest price they would pay for the same mug. Sellers' prices are commonly about twice as high as buyers'. The standard "
        "explanation is loss aversion: giving up something one owns feels like a loss, and losses weigh more heavily than equivalent gains.\n\n"
        "Later research added a qualification. When goods are held for exchange rather than for use, as when a shop owner sells stock or a "
        "trader sells shares, the effect largely disappears. A dealer does not mourn each sale. This finding suggests that the endowment effect "
        "depends less on ownership itself than on how the owner expects to use what is owned.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P4", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Coral reefs are often described as fragile, and in one sense they are: a few weeks of unusually warm water can cause corals to expel "
        "the algae that live in their tissues and supply most of their food, leaving the corals white and starving. Yet bleaching is not always "
        "fatal. If temperatures fall quickly enough, many corals take up algae again and recover. Some researchers now study reefs that have "
        "survived repeated bleaching, hoping to learn whether the corals there host heat-tolerant algae or have themselves become more resistant.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P5", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Literary historians have tended to read the explosion of travel writing in eighteenth-century Britain as evidence of a newly curious "
        "public, eager for knowledge of distant places. The sheer number of travel books seems to support this view: by some counts, they "
        "outsold every category except religious works. Yet a closer look at how such books were produced complicates the picture. Many were "
        "compiled by writers who had never left England, stitched together from earlier accounts and reissued under new titles with little "
        "revision. Publishers, it appears, were responding less to a demand for new information than to a demand for a familiar kind of reading "
        "pleasure: the narrative of a journey, with its promise of hardship overcome and its comfortable return home.\n\n"
        "None of this means readers were incurious. But it suggests that the popularity of a genre is weak evidence of the motives of its "
        "audience. A book can sell because it confirms expectations as readily as because it challenges them, and the travel book, in its most "
        "commercial forms, seems to have done more of the former than the latter.")},
    {"id": "GRE-T1-P6", "title": "Passage", "text": (
        "Many plants that flower in early spring, before the trees above them leaf out, are pollinated by queen bumblebees, which emerge from "
        "hibernation in the same weeks. Because flowering is triggered largely by temperature and bee emergence partly by day length, warming "
        "springs have prompted concern that the two events could drift apart, leaving early flowers unvisited. Records from one long-studied "
        "meadow suggest the concern is partly warranted: over forty years, the flowers there have advanced by about two weeks, the bees by only "
        "one. Yet seed production in the meadow has not declined, perhaps because late-season visits by other insects make up some of the difference.")},
]

